WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:12.660
9. The Challenger Tragedy. On January 28, 1986, thousands of people watched the launch

2
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:19.780
of the Challenger space shuttle on television. Many were students in classrooms, like I was.

3
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.860
Challenger stood on the launch pad, rocket boosters underneath it lit up, and Challenger

4
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:32.740
shot up into the blue sky. We students watched. Then, just over a minute after takeoff, Challenger

5
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:38.220
broke apart. Streams of smoke spilled from it, and the shuttle fell in pieces into the

6
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:45.380
ocean. People experienced a tragedy that day, and at NASA, the National Aeronautics and

7
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:52.440
Space Administration, experts wanted to learn what went wrong. Challenger had minor problems

8
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:58.480
from the start, but the disaster was caused by something called an O-ring. The O-ring

9
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.120
was supposed to protect Challenger from the hot gas of a rocket booster. But the cold

10
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.980
weather on the morning of the launch stopped the O-ring from working. Gases and flames

11
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:16.320
burned through one fuel tank as the shuttle lifted off. Stronged winds helped the flames.

12
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.280
A fuel tank and a rocket booster collided. The fuel exploded and tore the Challenger

13
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:29.360
apart. Bad mechanics and bad weather both contributed, but humans could have stopped

14
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:36.440
this disaster. NASA investigators learned that engineers knew the O-ring was flawed.

15
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.360
It should have been redesigned, but its flaws weren't reported. Instead, the flaws were

16
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:48.980
accepted. Investigators found that engineers only told the O-ring designer about its problems,

17
00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:55.180
not their NASA managers. This was against the rules, but the safety culture at NASA

18
00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:02.940
discouraged talking about problems. The Challenger disaster teaches us to speak up. Problems

19
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:09.860
must be faced, not avoided, even if talking about them is unpopular. When people are afraid

20
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:17.100
to say anything but, yes, terrible accidents can happen. NASA's new regulations are designed

21
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.660
to prevent that. Let's hope we've learned our lesson from Challenger.
